.TH CLINIC\-TRIAGE 1 "2026-09-07" "clinic-triage 1.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
clinic\-triage \- file clinic notes from the Taildrop inbox into sardinetracker
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clinic\-triage
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Moves clinical PDFs that a phone has sent by Taildrop into the sardinetracker
document library, proposing a description for each one and filing only the
descriptions you approve.
.PP
The work happens in four gates. A content hash decides whether the document is
new. A structural classifier decides whether it is a clinical record. Regular
expressions read whatever can be read directly off the page. A local language
model, running on this machine, is then asked only for the parts that need
judgement: a title, a document type, a specialty, and a one\- or two\-sentence
summary.
.PP
Nothing reaches the server without an explicit
.B y
at the review step, and nothing leaves this machine before that. The
describe step uses a model served locally by
.BR ollama (1);
clinic notes are not sent to a hosted service.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B get
Collect files the phone has sent, into the inbox. Runs
.B tailscale file get
with
.BR \-\-conflict=rename .
.TP
.B check
Classify and extract without calling the model. Milliseconds rather than
minutes, and the loop to work in when adjusting the classifier.
.TP
.B run
.B get
followed by
.BR describe .
The ordinary entry point.
.TP
.B describe
Describe documents the manifest has not seen. Roughly a minute each.
.TP
.B preview
Show exactly what would be filed, field by field, and send nothing.
.TP
.B push
Review each proposal and file the approved ones. Prompts
.BR y / n / e / q
per document;
.B e
opens the proposal in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.B status
List what is sitting in the queue.
.TP
.B roster
Re\-fetch the clinician list from the server, then recompute provider and
facility across the queue. Run this after adding a clinician.
.TP
.B repair
Recompute date, provider and facility for everything queued, leaving the
model's fields untouched. Use after changing an extractor \(em the expensive
half of the work is already paid for.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that poppler, tailscale, the inbox, ollama and the server are all
reachable, and that the token is accepted.
.
.SH THE PIPELINE
.nf
phone  \(-> Taildrop \(-> ~/Downloads/taildrop/
                              |
                              +\- hash        seen this before?
                              +\- classify    is it a clinical record?
                              +\- extract     date, provider, facility
                              +\- describe    title, type, specialty, summary
                              |
                         queue/*.json   \(em you read these
                              |
                              +\- push       you approve, one at a time
                              |
              POST /api/clinical/document \(-> /clinical#documents
.fi
.PP
Files flow one way. Nothing can pull from the phone: iOS runs no service a peer
can reach, so every arrival is a push the phone initiates, whether by hand from
the share sheet or by a Shortcuts automation.
.
.SH WHAT IS HELD BACK
The classifier answers whether a document has the shape of a clinical record.
It cannot answer whether you want your clinicians reading it. Four mechanisms
cover the difference, and each writes its reason into the queue.
.TP
.B excluded
Matched a filename glob in
.IR config.json .
Checked before anything else. Complaint drafts, briefs, letters.
.TP
.B not_clinical
Tripped a veto \(em journal article, legal filing, insurance paperwork, a
document written by the patient rather than a clinician, or a work document.
Or it carries no patient or encounter structure at all.
.TP
.B not_a_record
The model declined to type it as a clinical document. Blank intake paperwork
lands here.
.TP
.B too_short , needs_ocr
Too little text to judge, or no text layer at all. Never described, never sent.
.PP
Documents the patient wrote are the awkward case: they are about clinical
findings, they use clinical vocabulary, and they score as records. One was
typed by the model as a clinic note with a summary in a clinician's voice. That
is the version of the mistake that survives review, so it has a rule of its
own rather than relying on the classifier.
.
.SH FIELDS AND WHERE THEY COME FROM
Every proposal records the provenance of each field, so a wrong value can be
traced to the thing that produced it.
.TP
.B date
A labelled date in the text \(em
.IR collected ,
.I date of service
\(em otherwise the filename. There is deliberately no "earliest date in the
document" fallback: it returned a date of birth as the date of service. If the
filename's date appears nowhere in the document the field is marked
.BR "NOT corroborated" .
.TP
.B provider , facility
The clinician roster first, then a labelled field validated to look like a
name. Both return nothing rather than a plausible fragment; a wrong provider is
worse than a blank one in a record shown to clinicians.
.TP
.B title , doc_type , specialty , summary
The model. It is instructed to use the document's own wording for any finding
and to return nothing rather than invent. Patient identifiers are then stripped
from its output regardless of what it was told, because an instruction is not
a control.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/projects/clinic-triage/config.json
Server, API token, patient names to scrub, exclusion globs. Not in version
control.
.TP
.I ~/projects/clinic-triage/state/manifest.json
Content hash of every document seen, and its verdict. Delete an entry to make
one document new again; delete the file to re\-triage everything.
.TP
.I ~/projects/clinic-triage/state/clinicians.json
Cached clinician roster. Refreshed by
.BR "clinic\-triage roster" .
.TP
.I ~/projects/clinic-triage/queue/
One proposal per document, awaiting review.
.TP
.I ~/projects/clinic-triage/filed/
Proposals already filed, with the server's response recorded.
.TP
.I ~/Downloads/taildrop/
The inbox.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLINIC_TRIAGE_HOME
Project directory. Default
.IR ~/projects/clinic-triage .
.TP
.B CLINIC_TRIAGE_INBOX
Inbox directory. Default
.IR ~/Downloads/taildrop .
.TP
.B EDITOR
Used by
.B push
when you answer
.BR e .
.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B "403 Forbidden"
A proxy, not your token. Cloudflare rejects the default
.B Python\-urllib/3.x
User\-Agent before the request reaches the application, so it looks like an
auth failure and is not one. Both clients send a real User\-Agent. If you see
this from your own
.BR curl (1),
add one.
.TP
.B "302 to /login"
The endpoint is missing from the application's
.B require_login
whitelist. A token\-authenticated route that is not listed there redirects
instead of running, and nothing errors.
.TP
.B "401 unauthorized"
This one really is the token. Compare
.I api_token
in
.I config.json
against the server's.
.TP
.B "Access denied: file access denied"
.B tailscale file get
cannot reach the daemon as you. Fix it once with
.PP
.RS 7
.nf
sudo tailscale set \-\-operator=$USER
.fi
.RE
.IP
rather than running it under
.BR sudo (8),
which leaves every collected file owned by root.
.TP
.B "an error naming the target directory"
A file of that name is already in the inbox. Taildrop's default is to skip
conflicts, leave the file in its queue and print an error. Nothing is lost \(em
.B clinic\-triage get
passes
.B \-\-conflict=rename
for this reason.
.TP
.B "another triage is running"
Two runs share a manifest and a queue, and the second overwrites the first,
including replacing finished descriptions with un\-described ones. The message
names the process; if it is genuinely dead, remove
.IR state/triage.lock .
.
.SH CAVEATS
Filing is idempotent \(em the server rejects a duplicate by content hash, so a
retry after a dropped connection is safe.
.PP
If a document is already filed and the proposal describes it differently,
.B push
shows the difference and offers to correct the filed copy. Only fields the
proposal actually has are offered: one with no provider means "could not
determine one", so it will not blank a value entered by hand.
.B \-\-yes
does not overwrite;
.B \-\-update\-duplicates
applies changes without asking.
.PP
Extraction and classification were tuned against one person's inbox. The
structural rules should generalise; the veto thresholds are calibrated to that
corpus and would want re\-measuring elsewhere.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ordinary use:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
clinic\-triage run
clinic\-triage push
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what a new batch would be classified as, without spending model time:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
clinic\-triage get
clinic\-triage check
.fi
.RE
.PP
After adding a clinician to the record:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
clinic\-triage roster
.fi
.RE
.PP
File a single document by the start of its hash:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
clinic\-triage push \-\-only f84e7312
.fi
.RE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sardinetracker (7),
.BR tailscale (1),
.BR pdftotext (1),
.BR ollama (1)
.PP
The project README carries the reasoning behind the classifier and the
extractors, including the failures each rule was written against.
